02:38:05 anthropic: gave up: I couldn't get any metric to the 1% minimum, so I'm not submitting.

- **Attempt 1:** inlining the encode and decode loops into b58encode_check and b58decode_check made all three metrics worse: check_ir +0.46%, encode_ir +1.4%, decode_ir +1.2%. encode_ir rose even though its code path didn't change. My inference, not confirmed: the module seems to be recompiled on every benchmark run, so each added line of code costs roughly 50k instructions on every metric. Comparing check_ir with encode_ir suggests the inlining saved about 2M instructions of runtime, but the extra lines cost more than that.
- **Attempt 2:** removing comments only (behaviour-neutral) gave about 0.1%.
- **Attempt 3:** I made three more behaviour-preserving simplifications: a single plain loop for the rarely used fallback in b58decode_int, b58encode_int reusing the shared encode tables, and dropping the itertools import. Best result was check_ir 0.9956, encode_ir 0.9965, decode_ir 0.9970. Equivalence was identical, but every metric is still short of 0.99.

Python startup seems to account for most of the measured instructions, and the hot paths are already heavily optimised. The remaining idea, divide-and-conquer conversion for long inputs, would add enough code that the compile cost would likely cancel out what it saves. I still had one evaluation left but no change likely to clear 1%, so I stopped.
